  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+2z=k \\\\ x+y=-1 \\\\ y+z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $y=-5$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $1,$ find the value of $k.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1-0)-k(1-0)+2(2-0) \\\\  & =-2-k+4 \\\\  & =2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{2-k}{1}=2-k.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $y=-5.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-k & =-5 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
